  "hoods": {
    "Downtown": "The lights flicker back on along Broadway after an unexplained outage, workers in the Neon Terrace bar shrug and order another round. A new lease sign goes up above SpeedyBurger, the paper still crisp in the display case.",
    "Temescal": "The line at the health center stretches around the corner by midmorning, regulars leaning against the brick wall of Telegraph Avenue to wait their turn. A handwritten sign taped to the door lists the new intake hours.",
    "Laurel": "A quiet week — nothing much out of the ordinary around the neighborhood.",
    "West Oakland": "New faces keep arriving on the block—someone unloading a U-Haul by West Side Cafe nods at the regulars smoking outside. The club crawl posters are fresh on the telephone poles, bright against the industrial grays.",
    "Fruitvale": "Plans for the transit hub expansion sit in the window of a storefront office, pages curling at the edges from the sun. The taqueria down the block is busier than ever at lunch, mostly people in work boots and people in blazers.",
    "Jack London": "A cargo ship delay means the usual bustle along the waterfront is quieter, just a few stragglers nursing beers at the Blue Lantern. Someone says Pixel Pete was here last night, but nobody's sure if it's true.",
    "Rockridge": "The Dillon family's block party takes over the street, kids weaving through folding chairs while the grill sends up smoke. The smell of burgers lingers even after the last guest leaves.",
    "Adams Point": "A quiet week — nothing much out of the ordinary around the neighborhood.",
    "Grand Lake": "An older couple leaves the senior lunch at Or Hashalom, carrying leftovers in foil. The theater marquee hasn’t changed in weeks, same as the quiet storefronts along the strip.",
    "Piedmont Ave": "A quiet week — nothing much out of the ordinary around the neighborhood.",
    "Chinatown": "Flyers for the health fair flutter in the breeze outside the Buddhist temple, a stack already running low. The neighborhood panel meets in the back room of the cultural center, voices murmuring through the closed door.",
    "Brooklyn": "Foot traffic is lighter than usual, a few empty tables outside the cafes. The new apartments still look crisp, but the sidewalks feel too wide for the people on them.",
    "Eastlake": "A flicker, then the streetlights come back—someone on their porch claps once, half-sarcastic. The bodega’s neon sign buzzes back to life, casting pink light on the wet pavement.",
    "Glenview": "A quiet week — nothing much out of the ordinary around the neighborhood.",
    "Dimond": "Romano’s is busy as ever, the same regulars at the counter, the same red sauce simmering. A faded mural on the side of the building still shows the old name, the paint flaking at the edges.",
    "Ivy Hill": "A quiet week — nothing much out of the ordinary around the neighborhood.",
    "San Antonio": "A quiet week — nothing much out of the ordinary around the neighborhood.",
    "KONO": "The Northgate’s dim interior hides most faces, but someone swears they saw Sienna Vale in the corner booth. The Fox Theater’s marquee stays dark midweek, just the usual foot traffic passing by.",
    "Lake Merritt": "The lunch program at the Unitarian church wraps up, volunteers stacking chairs while a few seniors linger over coffee. A moving truck idles outside a Craftsman, the new tenant directing the crew.",
    "Uptown": "Another lease sign joins the row above the bars, the paper crisp and bright. The usual crowd spills out of the clubs, laughter bouncing off the new glass towers.",
